      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a very practical breakdown of the social friction points. It's clear that the \"dynamic\" guests require a much higher level of active management to prevent them from overwhelming the environment.\n\nTo take this a step further for our security and risk assessment protocols, I want to dig into the more sensitive side of these demographics. In our experience, when these high-visibility, \"new money\" groups travel, they often come in much larger, less predictable cohorts—sometimes involving large numbers of staff, security, or extended family members, which can look quite different from the predictable, small groups we see from our European legacy clients.\n\nCan you help me outline how these different \"social energies\" might correlate with specific types of operational risk? For instance, when we see these larger, more demonstrative groups—particularly those from regions where social structures are more communal or less focused on Western individualist privacy—is there a statistically higher tendency for issues like unauthorized guests in rooms, \"boisterous\" behavior that borders on unruly, or even certain types of illicit activity (like unauthorized large-scale parties or substance-related disturbances)? We need to help our security leads understand if certain cultural \"styles\" of group travel are inherently higher risk for these specific violations.",
